!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/policy.tn?sha256=402371919ea91ef1e9fa74569472a1515bb2456a819b355ff9a4d3e06e50e540"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=557fc58ae63efc942143760ac6a5dc3d77a176a04c65923e388e052e085ca317"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/core.tn?sha256=1eedf358c7bf28bd21b0ae6272e057be1ac11ffc6ddffd7b88839c54b7a909b7"
@doc:"""
  TSON processor policy — what a processor admits and spends (2026 Revision 39
  draft).

  A processor's policy is a property of the deployment, never of a schema
  ([TSON-DATA] §8.2): a schema that declared its own strictness would choose
  its own check. This document declares the policy's vocabulary, so a
  processor states the policy it judged under in one shape, and a deployment
  that writes its policy down writes a data document of type `policy`. Such a
  document is named at the call site and never resolved by identity, and no
  document may name, import or otherwise select the policy it is judged under.

  `identifier_policy` and `token_policy` are §8.2's two policies, and `limits`
  is §9.1's and [TSON-SCHEMA] §11.5's. A script is named by its Unicode Script property value alias
  (UAX #24), `Latin`, `Cyrillic`, `Han`.
  """
{
  @doc:"UTS #39 §5.2's restriction levels, strictest first."
  restriction_level => !enum [
    ASCII_ONLY SINGLE_SCRIPT HIGHLY_RESTRICTIVE MODERATELY_RESTRICTIVE MINIMALLY_RESTRICTIVE UNRESTRICTED
  ]

  @doc:"""
    [TSON-DATA] §8.2's token policy: the restriction level over each whole
    token, and any script combinations admitted over the level (UTS #39 §5.2's
    own device for Latn+Jpan and its siblings), each entry in `permitting` one
    such combination.
    """
  script_policy => { level: restriction_level  permitting: [[text]] }

  @doc:"""
    [TSON-DATA] §8.2's identifier policy: the restriction level, whether it
    applies to each segment of a name rather than to the whole of it, whether
    no two names in one scope may share a UTS #39 skeleton, and any script
    combinations admitted over the level, as `script_policy` states them.
    """
  identifier_policy => {
    level: restriction_level
    per_segment: boolean
    skeleton_distinctness: boolean
    permitting: [[text]]
  }

  @doc:"""
    [TSON-DATA] §9.1's resource limits and [TSON-SCHEMA] §11.5's: what a
    processor will spend reading one document, and resolving one schema, as
    against what it admits as a name. Every limit defaults to the spec's own
    value, set at the tight end so that a document fitting it fits every
    processor above, and a deployment writes only what it raises. A limit
    refusal names the limit and the threshold, and is not a verdict on the
    document.

    The §9.1 limits bound a document, a schema document among them. Sizes in
    code points, octets or bytes are `int64`; counts and depths are `int32`.
    The §11.5 limits bound the work resolution adds over a schema: the
    schemas one header reaches through `!!meta` and `!!import`, the
    declarations in one schema map (synthetic and instantiation entries
    included), the hops from a use site to a terminal entry, the length of
    one type's supertype chain, and the nested open synthetics closed for
    one application. A data document's own scope pushes are counted under
    `max_foreign_schemas`, on the document side.
    """
  limits => {
    max_depth?:                 int32 ~ 64
    max_token_length?:          int64 ~ 1048576
    max_text_length?:           int64 ~ 1048576
    max_numeric_literal_length?: int32 ~ 4096
    max_binary_size?:           int64 ~ 16777216
    max_document_size?:         int64 ~ 16777216
    max_elements?:              int32 ~ 1048576
    max_entries?:               int32 ~ 1048576
    max_fields?:                int32 ~ 65536
    max_annotations?:           int32 ~ 64
    max_values?:                int32 ~ 16777216
    max_foreign_schemas?:       int32 ~ 16
    max_import_closure?:        int32 ~ 64
    max_schema_entries?:        int32 ~ 65536
    max_reference_chain?:       int32 ~ 64
    max_supertype_chain?:       int32 ~ 64
    max_materialisation_depth?: int32 ~ 64
  }

  @doc:"""
    The policy a processor judges a document under: §8.2's identifier and
    token policies and §9.1's limits. `unicode_data_version` is the UCD release
    whose tables the §8.2 rules read, which a report states beside the policy
    (§8.2) and a deployment's own policy document leaves out, the tables being
    the processor's rather than the deployment's choice.
    """
  policy => {
    identifier_policy: identifier_policy
    token_policy: script_policy
    unicode_data_version?: text
    limits: limits
  }
}
